{
  "version": "registers-1",
  "note": "A REGISTER IS A NAMED REGION OF THE ATLAS, DEFINED BY A RULE — never a tag assigned per film. Assigned per film by a model these fragment (cerebral horror / intellectual horror / elevated horror / psychological horror: four labels, one idea, zero shared edges). Defined by a rule they cannot fragment, they are auditable (the terms that matched are right there), and they are correctable in one place. See docs/specs/atlas-fingerprint-layer-v1.1.md section C for the original 46-register list, and atlas/DESIGN.md for why this file defines them on THEMES rather than on axes.\n\nWHAT A RULE MAY READ. Themes (TMDB keywords, evidenced by a plot summary) and the recorded facets discovery.json already carries: genre, movement, era, country. It may NOT read degree, fame, poster presence, year of release as a ranking, or anything else AGENTS rule 1 forbids. Every rule below is a membership test over evidence; none of them ranks.\n\nTHE LINE THAT DECIDES WHAT SURVIVES: a register lives if it is about WHAT A FILM IS ABOUT, and dies if it is about HOW A FILM BEHAVES. Slow cinema, near-wordless, talk-driven, deadpan, chamber drama, sun-bleached dread — measured, all dead here, because a plot summary never states a cutting rate. Those wait for the axis build and are deliberately absent rather than faked.",
  "treatmentNote": "EVERY REGISTER CARRIES A RENDERING TREATMENT, AND SAYS HOW MUCH OF IT IS MEASURED. At field scale a per-film colour carries no information — nearest-neighbour hue difference across the solved layout is 67.6 degrees against 69.0 for a random shuffle, a ratio of 0.979 — so 2,204 discs in 2,204 measured highlights is the generic rainbow AGENTS rule 5 was written to forbid, arriving through the front door. Colour at field scale is a data channel and has to carry the stratum. `hue`, `glow`, `scale`, `edge`, `mono` and `motion` below are that channel.\n\nThe honesty is the three-tier `tier`, and build-registers.js DECIDES IT BY MEASUREMENT rather than taking this file's word for it: it compares each register's mean member highlight in Oklab against the corpus mean, in units of the null SD of same-size random samples.\n\n  recorded  — the treatment reports a fact about the print itself (black and white).\n  amplified — the register's own films measurably separate in colour, and the shipped\n              hue is that measured direction with its chroma pushed. The gain ships.\n  authored  — the films do NOT separate; the colour was chosen. Said out loud.\n\n`hue` here is the AUTHORED proposal. A register that measures as separating has its hue replaced by the amplified measurement at build time, and the file records both. Rule 1 still binds absolutely: `scale` is uniform across every film in a register, so nothing on screen is ever larger for being better connected, and `motion.kind: pulse` moves every disc in unison for the same reason. `drift` and `flicker` take their phase from a film's index in the corpus, which is arbitrary and carries no graph information whatsoever.",
  "gates": {
    "minFilms": 8,
    "maxShare": 0.12,
    "maxFacetJaccard": 0.8
  },
  "motionKinds": {
    "still": "No idle animation at all. As expressive as a pulse, and free.",
    "pulse": "One slow swell, every disc in unison — a held breath, not a heartbeat per film.",
    "drift": "Each disc on its own slow phase, taken from its corpus index. A field that shimmers.",
    "flicker": "Fast, shallow, desynchronised. Gate flicker, not a strobe: depth stays under a quarter.",
    "twinkle": "Scintillation. Same per-index phase as drift, but two beats against each other so the rhythm never resolves — which is what makes a star read as a star and a lamp read as a lamp."
  },
  "skyNote": "`sky: true` is the one treatment that changes what the field IS rather than how it is coloured, and it is spent on ONE register on purpose. This view has called itself the constellation since it was built: the discs are stars, the edges are the lines drawn between them, and the whole conceit has been a metaphor the interface never admitted to. Cold science fiction is where it stops pretending — a real star field behind the films, scintillation on the films themselves, and a meteor across the frame from time to time. It is also the register that earned it: at 6.8 SD it is the largest measured palette separation in the corpus, so its blue is reportage rather than decoration, and the readout says so. Do not give this to a second register. It is legible precisely because nothing else does it.",

